body:
## Dependencies

Start after issues #1–#3 provide GitHub coordination, a bounded project buyer, and a specialized solver path. If one sponsor integration remains externally blocked, use the truthful fallback for implementation and keep the real exit criterion visibly incomplete.

## Product objective

Complete the first authentic economic cycle:

```text
owner/donor funds a software project through real Stripe sandbox Checkout
→ project agent reserves and publishes a useful bounty
→ solver agent discovers, claims, and submits verified work
→ accepted reward is settled exactly once
→ part reaches the solver operator’s Stripe test connected account
→ an explicitly retained part becomes agent operating credit
→ the solver spends that retained credit to fund useful work on its own project
```

This is the strongest “agents earn, spend, and run operations” story. The accounting must be truthful and must never count one dollar twice.

## First gate: close the existing Stripe blocker

Read `docs/chatgpt-pro-stripe-blocker-report.md`, `docs/stripe-sandbox.md`, and the current Stripe reconciliation code.

Before changing the economic model, complete or rerun the existing real sandbox evidence path and preserve safe compact artifacts:

```text
cs_... Checkout Session
pi_... PaymentIntent
ch_... Charge
evt_... signed webhook event
acct_... test connected account
tr_... Connect Transfer
```

Require:

```text
ledger_reconciled: true
remote_checked: true
remote_reconciled: true
remote mismatches: []
```

Never commit `sk_test_`, `rk_test_`, `whsec_`, Checkout URLs with sensitive state, or full unnecessary webhook payloads.

If the operator has not supplied credentials/account setup, finish all code and deterministic tests, record one exact blocker, and continue the remaining implementation. Do not invent Stripe IDs.

## Settlement allocation model

Add an explicit, owner-approved settlement policy. A recommended sandbox/demo shape is:

```text
reward:                    $25.00
external Connect Transfer: $20.00
retained operating credit:  $5.00
platform fee:               $0.00
```

General invariant:

```text
external_transfer + retained_operating_credit + platform_fee = accepted reward
```

Requirements:

- integer minor units only;
- allocation policy is configured before claim or settlement;
- the solver operator must explicitly permit retained credit;
- the agent cannot increase retention, fee, or reward;
- absent explicit retention consent, default to full external transfer;
- a retained credit is an internal platform liability/operating balance, not money in an AI-owned bank account;
- only the external portion creates a Stripe Connect Transfer;
- retained funds remain backed by platform funds and cannot simultaneously appear as externally transferred;
- settlement and all ledger movements are exactly-once;
- currency must match throughout;
- reversal/manual-review paths preserve accounting truth.

Add additive schema and ledger accounts for settlement allocation, solver operating available/reserved/spent, and linkage to accepted receipt and Stripe transfer.

## Solver operating budget policy

A solver operating balance may spend only through trusted capabilities and a configured policy:

```text
allowed projects/repositories
maximum spend per action
minimum remaining reserve
allowed action classes
human approval threshold
required verifier for funded work
optional paid-tool budget
```

The agent proposes an action; trusted code enforces the policy.

Primary supported spend action for this issue:

```text
transfer retained solver operating credit
→ treasury of an allowlisted project controlled by the same operator
→ reserve/publish a new machine-verifiable bounty
```

This must be an internal double-entry transfer, not a fake new Stripe payment.

Optional only after the primary cycle works:

- one genuine MPP/HTTP-402 paid API call; or
- one genuine Stripe Projects provisioning action needed by a bounty.

Do not provision SaaS or buy an API solely for sponsor screen time. Stripe Link CLI is not required and must not block the Spain-based demo.

## Second authentic project/bounty

After the first accepted Motoko settlement, have the solver agent use retained credit to fund a small, authentic issue in an allowlisted project it operates—preferably `agent-bounty-market` itself or another user repository.

Requirements:

- issue is useful, narrow, and deterministic;
- project agent #2 evaluates and publishes it through issue #1’s GitHub event spine;
- retained credit is moved to that project treasury and reserved once;
- the second bounty contract and funding source visibly reference retained earnings from the first accepted bounty;
- no circular self-approval: a separate trusted verifier controls the second bounty;
- do not invent a trivial typo only for the demo.

If no suitable authentic second issue exists, create a clearly labeled benchmark fixture and leave the “authentic recursive spend” criterion incomplete.

## Integrated state machine

Extend the end-to-end state so the following are durable and replay-safe:

```text
Stripe funding request
→ signed funding event
→ project available balance
→ bounty reservation/publication
→ claim/submission/verification
→ accepted reward allocation
→ external transfer pending/completed
→ retained operating credit available
→ second project funding/reservation
```

Every cross-system operation needs a stable idempotency key, parameters digest, remote object ID where applicable, and recovery for “remote succeeded, local write failed.”

## Real integrated demo command

Add one orchestrated command, for example:

```bash
python -m agent_bounty demo-economic-loop \
  --db .demo/economic-loop.sqlite3 \
  --motoko-repo /home/mares/repos/motoko-issue-1-tui-input-latency
```

It may pause for hosted Checkout/browser completion, but must require no manual database edits.

The output/timeline must show:

1. real or explicitly fake funding provider;
2. project-agent decision and policy approval;
3. GitHub issue/contract URL and digest;
4. solver-agent declines/claim decision;
5. PR/candidate SHA;
6. protected receipt;
7. reward allocation;
8. Stripe `tr_...` for only the external portion;
9. retained operating balance;
10. retained-credit transfer to second project;
11. second funded bounty URL/digest;
12. replay flags and ledger/remote reconciliation.

## P&L and intelligence-per-dollar metrics

Record and display conservatively:

```text
reward
external transfer
retained operating credit
platform fee
model/tool/sandbox estimated cost
observed elapsed time
verified outcome count
gross margin estimate
cost per accepted bounty
```

Label estimates as estimates. Do not invent model-token or infrastructure costs when unavailable.

## Tests

Add deterministic tests for at least:

1. settlement components do not sum to reward;
2. negative or cross-currency allocation;
3. retention without operator consent;
4. full-transfer default;
5. external and retained portions cannot double-count;
6. transfer failure/retry/reversal;
7. crash after remote transfer before local finalization;
8. replay of accepted settlement;
9. operating spend above policy;
10. operating balance insufficient;
11. retained credit funds allowlisted project once;
12. retained credit cannot fund arbitrary external repo;
13. second bounty requires protected verifier;
14. project-agent/solver-agent prompt injection cannot change allocation;
15. first rejection grants no earning or operating credit;
16. real Stripe sandbox integration, gated;
17. full fake-provider economic loop;
18. process restart preserves all idempotency;
19. remote Stripe reconciliation matches split transfer;
20. no secrets in logs, traces, GitHub comments, receipts, or demo JSON.

## Documentation

Update architecture, threat model, Stripe runbook, Hermes handoff, and demo flow. Explain clearly:

- Checkout payment;
- internal project treasury;
- accepted reward allocation;
- Connect Transfer to a connected account balance;
- retained internal operating credit;
- agent spending that credit;
- bank payout outside the system.

## Non-goals

Do not implement:

- live-mode payments;
- legal escrow claims;
- public tax/KYC/dispute automation;
- arbitrary wallet custody;
- unbounded agent procurement;
- large marketplace UI (issue #5 handles presentation).

## External-blocker rule

If Stripe, GitHub, Hermes, or OpenShell credentials remain unavailable, complete the full deterministic loop, status commands, exact setup docs, and blocker report, then continue to issue #5. A fake path must be labeled fake in every output. Do not stop the queue waiting for the operator.

## Completion gate

- one accepted reward is split without double-counting;
- one real Connect Transfer exists when sandbox setup permits;
- retained credit funds a second project/bounty exactly once;
- full replay and reconciliation are clean;
- real versus fake integrations are unmistakable;
- default CI is secret-free and green;
- push focused commits to `origin/main`;
- add a handoff comment here with commits, safe Stripe/GitHub IDs, integrated demo JSON, recursive-spend status, blockers, and a pointer to issue #5.
